      "obligation": "refuter:12:1",
      "id": "def-riesz-potential-of-order-alpha",
      "location": "Definition — Scope of the definition",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "The text says the endpoint cases “cannot be added to that existence statement.” Read literally, this is false for p=1: for any f in L^1 and bounded ball B, Tonelli gives integral_B integral K_alpha(x-y)|f(y)| dy dx = integral |f(y)| integral_B K_alpha(x-y) dx dy. The inner integral is uniformly bounded in y because |z|^(alpha-n) is locally integrable for alpha>0 and bounded on distant translates. Thus the absolute potential is finite almost everywhere on every bounded ball, hence almost everywhere on R^n. The p=1 counterexample refutes the strong L^1-to-L^{n/(n-alpha)} estimate, not the a.e. existence clause.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "1c889eec6eda17a976b2c9d249fff20984cc3192760aa2671bb957cfd6c9dc52"
    },
    {
      "obligation": "refuter:12:2",
      "id": "thm-hardy-littlewood-sobolev-fractional-integration",
      "location": "1.1 The exponents",
      "defect": "false-claim",
      "evidence": "Step 1.1 defines theta=alpha p/n but asserts “theta q=alpha q/n.” For the valid parameters n=3, alpha=1, p=2, q=6, theta q=4 while alpha q/n=2. The identity should be theta q=alpha p q/n=q-p.",
      "severity": "fatal",
      "observed_sha256": "3a9f4eb4e8dbca5f23ae5677fbda10c53fa2caa13b4b562dca22548887522441"
